{"name":"Montezuma","hex":"#d2cdb6","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Montezuma","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A Nahuatl male given name, the name of two Aztec emperors.","Moctezuma II (1466–1520), the Aztec emperor at the time of the Spanish conquest."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Montezuma"}],"entries":[{"title":"Moctezuma I","blocks":[{"text":"Moctezuma I, also known as Montezuma I, Moteuczomatzin Ilhuicamina or Huehuemoteuczoma, was the second Aztec emperor and fifth king of Tenochtitlan. During his reign, the Aztec Empire was consolidated, major expansion was undertaken, and Tenochtitlan started becoming the dominant partner of the Aztec Triple Alliance. Often mistaken for his popular descendant, Moctezuma II, Moctezuma I greatly contributed to the famed Aztec Empire that thrived until Spanish arrival. He ruled over a period of peace from 1440 to 1453. Moctezuma brought social, economical, and political reform to strengthen Aztec rule, and Tenochtitlan benefited from relations with other cities."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/Huehuemoteuczoma_mendoza.jpg/330px-Huehuemoteuczoma_mendoza.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moctezuma_I","wikidata":"Q13702"},{"title":"Moctezuma II","blocks":[{"text":"Moctezuma Xocoyotzin, retroactively referred to in European sources as Moctezuma II, and often called Montezuma, was the ninth emperor of the Aztec Empire, reigning from 1502 or 1503 to 1520. Through his marriage with Queen Tlapalizquixochtzin of Ecatepec, one of his two wives, he was also the king consort of the altepetl."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Moctezuma_Mendoza.jpg/330px-Moctezuma_Mendoza.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moctezuma_II","wikidata":"Q141791"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/Moctezuma_I%2C_the_Fifth_Aztec_King.png/500px-Moctezuma_I%2C_the_Fifth_Aztec_King.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/Moctezuma_I%2C_the_Fifth_Aztec_King.png/1280px-Moctezuma_I%2C_the_Fifth_Aztec_King.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/Moctezuma_I%2C_the_Fifth_Aztec_King.png/500px-Moctezuma_I%2C_the_Fifth_Aztec_King.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The coronation of Moctezuma I[8]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMoctezuma_I%2C_the_Fifth_Aztec_King.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/War_between_Tenochtitlan_and_Chalco.jpg/500px-War_between_Tenochtitlan_and_Chalco.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/War_between_Tenochtitlan_and_Chalco.jpg/500px-War_between_Tenochtitlan_and_Chalco.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"War between Tenochtitlan and Chalco[15]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWar_between_Tenochtitlan_and_Chalco.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Aztecexpansion.png/500px-Aztecexpansion.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Aztecexpansion.png/1280px-Aztecexpansion.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Aztecexpansion.png/500px-Aztecexpansion.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Map showing the expansion of the Aztec empire showing the areas conquered by the Aztec rulers. The conquests of Moctezuma is marked by the colour pink.[19]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAztecexpansion.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Moctezuma_II.jpg/500px-Moctezuma_II.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Moctezuma_II.jpg/500px-Moctezuma_II.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"1892 illustration of Moctezuma II","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMoctezuma_II.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Moctezuma%27s_feather_headdress%2C_ca._1515%2C_Mexico%3B_Weltmuseum%2C_Vienna_%283%29.jpg/500px-Moctezuma%27s_feather_headdress%2C_ca._1515%2C_Mexico%3B_Weltmuseum%2C_Vienna_%283%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Moctezuma%27s_feather_headdress%2C_ca._1515%2C_Mexico%3B_Weltmuseum%2C_Vienna_%283%29.jpg/1280px-Moctezuma%27s_feather_headdress%2C_ca._1515%2C_Mexico%3B_Weltmuseum%2C_Vienna_%283%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Moctezuma%27s_feather_headdress%2C_ca._1515%2C_Mexico%3B_Weltmuseum%2C_Vienna_%283%29.jpg/500px-Moctezuma%27s_feather_headdress%2C_ca._1515%2C_Mexico%3B_Weltmuseum%2C_Vienna_%283%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Headdress traditionally attributed to Moctezuma II in the Museum of Ethnology, Vienna. The object, however, might not have belonged to Moctezuma and, contrary to popular belief, it was not used as his crown. It might have been a religious headdress to represent the god Quetzalcoatl during religious events, a battle standard, or a military device worn in the back.[7]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMoc